message.content:
Looking at the timer observations, `OverheatTimer.ET` is always 0 and `OverheatTimer.Q` never becomes TRUE even after 5001ms. The root cause is the initialization line at the start:

```st
OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#5S);
CooldownTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#3S);
```

This resets both timers to IN=FALSE every scan, wiping out accumulated elapsed time. IEC TON function blocks maintain state between calls and must be called exactly once per scan with the computed IN value, not reset at the beginning.

```json
{
  "edits": [
    {
      "old": "(* Initialize timers on first scan *)
OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#5S);
CooldownTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#3S);

(* PUB004: Sensor fault detection - immediate heater disable *)
tmp_SensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);

(* Primary control logic with safety priority *)
IF tmp_SensorFault THEN
    (* PUB004: Sensor fault - disable heater, enter protection state *)
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    OverheatProtection := TRUE;
    OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE);
    CooldownTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#3S);
    IF CooldownTimer.Q THEN
        OverheatProtection := FALSE;
        CooldownTimer(IN := FALSE);
    END_IF;

ELSIF OverheatProtection THEN
    (* PUB005, PUB008: Overheat protection active - heater forced OFF, cooldown in progress *)
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    CooldownTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#3S);
    OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE);
    IF CooldownTimer.Q THEN
        OverheatProtection := FALSE;
        CooldownTimer(IN := FALSE);
    END_IF;

ELSE
    (* Normal temperature control *)
    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN
        HeaterOutput := TRUE;
    ELSE
        HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    END_IF;

    (* PUB005, PUB006: Overheat protection - track 5s continuous heater operation *)
    IF HeaterOutput THEN
        OverheatTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#5S);
        IF OverheatTimer.Q THEN
            (* PUB002, PUB008: Overheat triggered - auto-off heater, start 3s cooldown *)
            OverheatProtection := TRUE;
            HeaterOutput := FALSE;
            OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE);
            CooldownTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#3S);
        END_IF;
    ELSE
        OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE);
    END_IF;
END_IF;",
      "new": "(* PUB004: Sensor fault detection - immediate heater disable *)
tmp_SensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);

(* Compute timer enable conditions first, then call timers once per scan *)
IF tmp_SensorFault THEN
    tmp_OverheatIN := FALSE;
    tmp_CooldownIN := TRUE;
    tmp_HeaterCmd := FALSE;
    tmp_StartOverheat := FALSE;
    tmp_ClearOverheat := FALSE;

ELSIF OverheatProtection THEN
    tmp_OverheatIN := FALSE;
    tmp_HeaterCmd := FALSE;
    tmp_StartOverheat := FALSE;
    tmp_CooldownIN := TRUE;
    tmp_ClearOverheat := CooldownTimer.Q;

ELSE
    (* Normal temperature control *)
    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN
        tmp_HeaterCmd := TRUE;
    ELSE
        tmp_HeaterCmd := FALSE;
    END_IF;

    tmp_CooldownIN := FALSE;
    tmp_ClearOverheat := FALSE;

    (* PUB005, PUB006: Overheat protection - track 5s continuous heater operation *)
    IF tmp_HeaterCmd THEN
        tmp_OverheatIN := TRUE;
        tmp_StartOverheat := OverheatTimer.Q;
    ELSE
        tmp_OverheatIN := FALSE;
        tmp_StartOverheat := FALSE;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

(* Call timers exactly once per scan with computed IN values *)
OverheatTimer(IN := tmp_OverheatIN, PT := T#5S);
CooldownTimer(IN := tmp_CooldownIN, PT := T#3S);

(* Apply outputs after timer calls *)
IF tmp_StartOverheat THEN
    (* PUB002, PUB008: Overheat triggered - auto-off heater, start 3s cooldown *)
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    OverheatProtection := TRUE;
ELSIF tmp_SensorFault THEN
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    OverheatProtection := TRUE;
ELSIF OverheatProtection THEN
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    IF tmp_ClearOverheat THEN
        OverheatProtection := FALSE;
    END_IF;
ELSE
    HeaterOutput := tmp_HeaterCmd;
END_IF;"
    }
  ]
}
```

message.reasoning_content:
Let me analyze the problem. The candidate is failing functional checks because:

1. The timer `OverheatTimer.Q` is never becoming TRUE even after 5+ seconds of operation
2. Looking at the timer observations, `OverheatTimer.ET` is always 0, which means the timer is not accumulating elapsed time

The issue is that the timer is being initialized every scan with:
```st
OverheatTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#5S);
```

This resets the timer every scan! The timer needs to be called only once per scan, not reset at the beginning. In IEC 61131-3, the TON function block has its state maintained between calls - you shouldn't re-initialize it every scan.

The correct pattern should be:
```st
OverheatTimer(IN := <condition>, PT := T#5S);
```

And then later, the `OverheatTimer.Q` will be evaluated. The issue is that in my implementation, I'm initializing it at the start of every scan with `IN := FALSE`.

Let me fix this by removing the initialization at the start and only setting `IN` when needed in the logic.

Also, I need to be more careful about the scan semantics. Looking at the public contract supplement:
- "On same-scan state changes determine the final timer enable before calling the timer so a newly enabled output and its elapsed-time origin agree."

This means I need to compute the timer enable condition first, then call the timer once with that final value.

Let me rewrite the logic to:
1. Compute all conditions first
2. Then call timers with the computed IN values
3. Use the timer Q values after the calls

Here's the corrected implementation:

```st
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB_TemperatureControl
VAR_INPUT
    TemperatureSensorInput : REAL;
    SetTemperature : REAL;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    CurrentTemperature : REAL;
    SetTempDisplay : REAL;
    HeaterStatus : BOOL;
    OverheatProtection : BOOL;
    HeaterOutput : BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR
    OverheatTimer : TON;
    CooldownTimer : TON;
    tmp_SensorFault : BOOL;
    tmp_OverheatTriggered : BOOL;
    tmp_OverheatIN : BOOL;
    tmp_CooldownIN : BOOL;
END_VAR

(* First, compute all conditions based on current inputs and state *)
tmp_SensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);

(* Determine heater control based on conditions *)
IF tmp_SensorFault THEN
    tmp_OverheatIN := FALSE;
    tmp_CooldownIN := TRUE;
    OverheatProtection := TRUE;
ELSIF OverheatProtection THEN
    tmp_OverheatIN := FALSE;
    tmp_CooldownIN := TRUE;
    HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    IF CooldownTimer.Q THEN
        OverheatProtection := FALSE;
        tmp_CooldownIN := FALSE;
    END_IF;
ELSE
    (* Normal operation *)
    tmp_CooldownIN := FALSE;
    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN
        HeaterOutput := TRUE;
        tmp_OverheatIN := TRUE;
    ELSE
        HeaterOutput := FALSE;
        tmp_OverheatIN := FALSE;
    END_IF;

    (* Check for overheat condition *)
    IF tmp_OverheatIN THEN
        OverheatProtection := TRUE;
        HeaterOutput := FALSE;
    END_IF;
END_IF;

(* Update timer states *)
OverheatTimer(IN := tmp_OverheatIN, PT := T#5S);
CooldownTimer(IN := tmp_CooldownIN, PT := T#3S);

(* Status outputs *)
CurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;
SetTempDisplay := SetTemperature;
HeaterStatus := HeaterOutput;
END_FUNCTION_BLOCK
```

The key fix is to only initialize timers once per scan with the computed input conditions, rather than resetting them at the start of every scan. This allows the TON timers to properly accumulate elapsed time and trigger their Q outputs. Looking at the overheat protection logic, I need to ensure the timer is properly managed across scans. The issue is that `OverheatTimer` gets reset every invocation since I'm calling it with `IN := FALSE` initially. 

The solution is to call `OverheatTimer` exactly once per scan with the correct `IN` value, then check its output afterward. I should compute the conditions first—checking if the heater is running and whether a sensor fault or protection state exists—then apply those to the timer call, and finally evaluate the timer output to set the protection flag. I need to complete the logic for the normal operation case and then finalize the function block. When the heater is on and the overheat timer reaches its limit, I should trigger overheat protection, disable the heater, and start the cooldown timer. Otherwise, if the heater is off, I disable the overheat timer. The status outputs get updated every scan to reflect the current temperature, set temperature, and heater status. The core problem is that initializing the timer at the start of each scan with `IN := FALSE` erases its accumulated state, preventing `OverheatTimer.Q` from ever becoming true. I need to restructure the logic to call each timer exactly once per scan, computing all conditions upfront so I can set the timer inputs correctly before invoking them.

